GOx as a typical oxido-reductase which can effectively catalyze glucose to \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and glucose acid, has attracted increasing research interest in bacterial disinfections (Li et al., 2021; Wang, Peng, et al., 2021). Based on the abundant glucose in vivo, the GOx-mediated consumption of physiological glucose could continuously supply \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and acidic pH induced by the generated glucose acid for triggering the POD-mimicking activity of POD mimics, which could overcome the pH limit and demand of exogenous \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) for POD-mimicking activity, as well as continuously produce exogenous •OH for bacteria inactivation. Considering glucose
widely exists in wound exudate (Trengove, Langton, & Stacey, 1996) and blood, it simultaneously provides nutrients for bacteria growth and promotes biofilm formation with high glucose level (Trulear & Characklis, 1982), which brings severe challenge for bacteria infectious wound healing. Typically, high blood glucose of diabetic patients leads to chronic ulcer of infected wounds (Hamid, Arbab, & Yousef, 2020). Based on the glucose-responsive antibacterial strategy, it can consume the glucose from wound blood and exudate for inhibiting the bacterial proliferation, and simultaneously trigger the production of •OH for bacteria killing.
Hydrogel dressings along with flexibility, noticeable water retention ability, excellent biocompatibility and antibacterial efficacy could be promising biomaterials for combating bacterial infections. Taking advantages of nanozymes in biocatalytic therapy, construction of nanozyme-based hydrogels with ROS regulation ability for biomedical application has attracted increasing research interest (Huang et al., 2021; Wang, Sun, et al., 2021; Wu, Wang, et al., 2019). The specific three-dimensional network structure endows hydrogel dressings with high encapsulation capacity to immobilize nanocatalysts for mediating ROS generation, long-term retention and sustainable release of encapsulated substances, as well as could significantly prevent the bacterial invasion. Moreover, since uncontrollable hemorrhage in surgery incisions and injured sites bring more difficulties for bacteria killing, hydrogel dressings can stop the undesired bleeding by absorbing blood which leads to the platelets aggregation and activation, and
subsequently activate the blood coagulation cascades for hemostasis (Liang, He, & Guo, 2021; Ssab et al., 2021). Simultaneously, the glucose in absorbed blood can be consumed by the GOx-mediated cascaded reaction for catalyzing the generation of •OH for antimicrobial defense, which can achieve synergistic hemostasis and bacteria inactivation.
Based on the above considerations, we aim to design a nanozyme encapsulated biomimetic hydrogel with glucose-responsive catalytic ability for synergistic antibacterial defense and hemostasis (as illustrated in Scheme. 1). A MOF-based biomimetic nanoreactor \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) was constructed by assembling GOx on the Cu ions doped graphited imidazolate framework (ZIF-8). Furthermore, the as-prepared \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) was encapsulated in a biocompatible hybrid hydrogel consisting of BC and guar gum (GG). Such a biomimetic hydrogel was designed by following the factors below: Based on the abundant glucose in vivo, glucose can be consumed by GOx to produce \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and glucose acid, subsequently triggers the Cu-mediated POD-mimicking activity of \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF}\) to produce high oxidative •OH for bacteria killing, which could address the pH limit and exogenous \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) demands of POD-mimicking activity for antibacterial application. The nanofibrous BC serves as backbones to reinforce the mechanical strength of the prepared hydrogel, as well as improves the water retention and absorption ability. Specifically, the dynamically crosslinked network with high water absorption capacity endows the prepared hydrogel with remarkable hemostatic ability for effective hemorrhage control by absorbing blood and triggering the blood coagulation cascades. Meanwhile, the absorbed
blood glucose could be converted to \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) by \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\)-mediated cascade reaction. Such design of glucose-responsive hydrogel with self-triggered biocatalytic function for synergistic hemostatic and antibacterial therapy brings new insight for nanozyme-based biocatalytic therapies.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Material
Terephthalic acid (TA), OG (food-grade extra refine, viscosity 5000–5500 cps) and \(3,3^{\prime}5,5^{\prime}\)-tetramethylbenzidine (TMB) were obtained from J&K Chemical Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Commercial BC suspension was purchased from Guilin Qihong Technology Co., Ltd. (Guilin, China). BC is white viscous liquid \((0.5\;\mathrm{wt}\%\) of the smashed BC suspended in aqueous solution) which was produced by Gluconacetobactor xylinus according to the statement of manufacturer. \(5,5^{\prime}\)-Dimethyl-1-pyrroline N-oxide (DMPO) was purchased from Accela Technology Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). \(5,5^{\prime}\)-Dithiobis (2-nitrobenzoic acid) (DTNB) and reduced GSH were purchased from Aladdin Reagents Co., Ltd. (Shanghai, China). Sodium borate, zinc nitrate hexahydrate, copper acetate monohydrate and 2-methylimidazole were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. (Shanghai, China). All chemicals were used as received.
## 2.2. Characterization
Fourier transform infrared (FTIR, Nicolet NEXUS 470 spectrometer), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Thermo ESCALAB 250Xi Spectrometer) and X-ray diffraction (XRD, Bruker D2 PHASER X-ray diffractometer) were applied to analyze the composition, structure and surface characteristic of the materials. Morphology of the materials was characterized using Transmission electron microscopy (TEM, JEOL JEM2100F) and field-emission scanning electron microscopy (SEM, JEOL S4800). Electron spin resonance (ESR) spectra was recorded with an EMXplus-10/12 ESR spectrometer (Bruker AXS).
## 2.3. Preparation of Cu@ZIF/GOx and BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx hydrogel
\(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF}\) was synthesized according to the previous report (Zhao, Nie, Wang, Chen, & Chen, 2020). Typically, \(5.94\:\mathrm{g}\) zinc nitrate hexahydrate and \(0.796\,\mathrm{~g~}\) copper acetate monohydrate were dissolved in \(100\,\mathrm{\mL}\) methanol. Then \(6.56\,\mathrm{~g~}\) 2-methylimidazole in \(100\,\mathrm{\mL}\) methanol was added in the above solution and stirred for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) . The mixture solution was heated at \(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(^{3\mathrm{~h~}}\) in a Teflon-lined autoclave. Then the products were washed with methanol and DMF and collected by centrifugation. The collected power was carbonized at \(1000\ {}^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\;\mathrm{{h}}\) with the heating rate of \(10\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min}\) under \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) atmosphere to obtain the final products of \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF}\) .
For leading with GOx, \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF}\) \((5~\mathrm{mg/mL})\) power was dispersed in water with ultrasound for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) . Subsequently, GOx \((5~\mathrm{mg/mL})\) and polyethylene glycol (PEG, \(2\ \mathrm{mg/mL})\) were added in the solution and stirred at room temperature for \(4\,\mathrm{~h~}\) . The products were collected by centrifugation and dried in vacuum, stored at \(4\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for future use. The sample was denoted as \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) .
To fabricate \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) encapsulated hydrogel, \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) (4 \(\mathrm{mg/mL})\) was ultrasonically dispersed in water, GG power \((1\ \mathrm{wt}\%)\) was dissolved in \(10\,\mathrm{g}\) of the above solution with stirring for \(20\;\mathrm{min}\) to form a viscous gel. Then \(10~\mathrm{g}\) of BC suspension was added in the mixture solution and stirred for \(30~\mathrm{\min}\) . BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx hydrogel was formed by adding \(400~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of sodium borate \(\mathrm{(NaBH_{4}}\) , \(100\ \mathrm{mM})\) in the mixture solution for crosslinking over a period of \(5\;\mathrm{min}\) . GG hydrogel and BC/GG hydrogel were prepared following the same crosslinking procedure.
## 2.4. POD-mimicking catalytic activity and GSH depletion activity of Cu@ZIF
The POD-mimicking activity of \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF}\) was investigated using TMB as the substrate. The reaction was performed in NaAc buffer \((0.1\;\mathrm{M},\,\mathrm{pH}\) \(=4)\) and \(\mathrm{O.D.652nm}\) was measured using a UV–vis spectrophotometer for analysis. For \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) detection, TA was used as fluorescence probe for tracking \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) , since \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) can cause the oxidation of TA to form 2-hydroxy terephthalic with the maximum fluorescence peak at \(435\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Ishibashi, Fujishima, Watanabe, & Hashimoto, 2000). \(1~\mathrm{mM}\) TA was mixed with the prepared nanocatalysts and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) \(\left(50\;\mathrm{mM}\right)\) in NaAc buffer \((0.1\mathrm{~M~}\) , \(\mathrm{pH}\!=\!4\!\!\!\) and incubated for \(^{1\,\mathrm{h}}\) . The emission fluorescence at \(435\,\mathrm{nm}\) with excitation wavelength at \(315\,\mathrm{\nm}\) was recorded using a fluorescence spectrophotometer for analysis. FSR spectra was applied to further identify the generated \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) using DMPO as spin-trapping adduct of \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) . \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF}\) \(\mathrm{[100~\mug/mL)}\) and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) \(\left(50\;\mathrm{mM}\right)\) were reacted in NaAc buffer \((0.1\;\mathrm{M},\mathrm{pH}\,4\) , containing \(100\,\mathrm{mM}\) DMPO) for \(30\,\mathrm{min}\) . The mixed solution was centrifugated, and supernatant was inserted in the ESR cavity for test.
The amount of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) catalyzed by \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) in the reaction solution was monitored using \(\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{2}\) as indicator. For \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) detection, groups of 1) GOx \((50\,\upmu\mathrm{g/mL)}\) , 2) \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF}\) \((50\,\upmu\mathrm{g/mL})\) + GOx \((50\,\upmu\mathrm{g/mL)}\) and 3) BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx \((300\ \upmu\mathrm{L/mL})\) were added in glucose solution \((10\,\mathrm{mM})\) with continuous stirring. At predetermined time intervals, \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) supernatant collected by centrifugation was mixed with \(2~\mathrm{mL}\) Ti \((\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{2}\) solution (containing \(0.8\%\) \(\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{2}\) and \(10\%\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4})\) . After 30 min, \(\mathrm{O.D.405\nm}\) was recorded using a UV–vis spectrophotometer.
GSH depletion activity of \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF}\) was evaluated using DTNB as the indicator of GSH (Shan, Li, Yang, Xiu, & Wang, 2019). Typically, \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF}\) \((50\ \upmu\mathrm{g/mL)}\) was added in GSH solution \((1\;\mathrm{mM})\) with stirring for \(4\;\mathrm{h}\) , followed by centrifuging at \(5000\ \mathrm{rpm}\) for \(5\;\mathrm{min}\) . The obtained supernatants were mixed with \(0.1\ \mathrm{mM}\) DTNB for \(5\;\mathrm{min}\) , \(\mathrm{O.D.412\nm}\) of the mixed solution was recorded using a UV–vis spectrophotometer.
