## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Preparation and characterization of Cu@ZIF
Cu@ZIF was synthesized by a hydrothermal method to obtain the Cu
doped precursor and following a carbonization process to obtain the final product as illustrated in Fig. 1a. The obtained \(\mathrm{Cu}@Z\mathrm{IF}\) exhibited a homogeneous rhombic dodecahedral morphology (Fig. 1b), which is similar to the morphology of the ZIF-8 prepared following the same procedure without the addition of copper ions (Fig. S1). Meanwhile, no metallic nanoparticles and clusters occurred on the surface of \(\mathrm{Cu}@Z\mathrm{IF}\) was observed from the TEM images in Figs. 1c, d, demonstrating the uniformly distribution of Cu. To identify the immobilization of Cu ions in the MOF structure, inductively coupled plasma mass spectrometry (ICP-MS) was applied to quantify the released Cu ions content from the aqueous solution of \(\mathrm{Cu}@Z\mathrm{IF}\) after continuous stirring for \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) . The result showed that extremely negligible amount of Cu ions was released from \(\mathrm{Cu}@Z\mathrm{IF}\) (Fig. S2), demonstrating the stable \(\mathrm{Cu}\) distribution. During the formation of \(\mathrm{Cu}@Z\mathrm{IF}\) precursor, copper ions were simultaneously incorporated in the nanoscaled ZIF-8 structure by interacting with the 2- methylmidazole, and subsequently Cu ions could be immobilized in the structure of \(\mathrm{Cu}@Z\mathrm{IF}\) by the following carbonization procedure, resulting in such a stable and homogeneous Cu distribution. After assembling with GOx, \(\mathrm{Cu}@Z\mathrm{IF}/\mathrm{GOx}\) showed a harsh surface with unconspicuous rhombic morphology (Fig. 1e), indicating the presence of GOx layer coated on the surface of \(\mathrm{Cu}@Z\mathrm{IF}\) . Furthermore, obvious characteristic peaks of amide I and amide II of GOx were observed in the FTIR spectra (Fig. S3a), further validated the presence of GOx.
The composition and valence states of \(\mathrm{Cu}@Z\mathrm{IF}\) were characterized by XRD and XPS. After high-temperature pyrolysis, \(\mathrm{Cu}@Z\mathrm{IF}\) showed only (002) and (101) diffraction peaks of graphitic carbon (Fig. 1f), and no crystal-phase diffraction peaks assigned to metallic Cu and its compounds were found, indicating the uniformly dispersed Cu ions in the MOF structure. Furthermore, XPS spectra in Fig. S3b presented clear C, N, O, and Cu element signals. Deconvoluted high-resolution XPS spectra of N1 (Fig. 1g) exhibited distinct peaks with binding energy at \(397.9\,\mathrm{eV}\) ,
398.7 eV, 399.9 eV and 401.3 eV, which were assigned to pyridinic N, Cu—N, pyrrolic N and graphite N, respectively (Lei et al., 2019; Zhao et al., 2020). These N species provide the coordination sites for the immobilization of Cu dispersion in \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF}\). Additionally, high-resolution XPS spectra of Cu 2p (Fig. 1h) showed two main spin-orbit peaks for Cu 2p1/2 and Cu 2p3/2 at 931.9 eV and 951.7 eV, respectively. The peaks located at 954.3 eV and 934.4 eV were attributed to \(\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\), and peaks at 951.6 eV and 931.8 eV belonged to \(\mathrm{Cu1^{+}}\), respectively (Xiong et al., 2021), demonstrating the coexistence of two different Cu species in \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF}\).
## 3.2. Crosslinking analysis and self-healing property of BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/ GOx hydrogel
BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx hydrogel was prepared using \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) to induce the crosslinking of the hydrogel precursor. As presented in Fig. 2a, \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) could quickly induce the crosslinking of the hydrogel. After lyophilization, the hydrogel presented irregular microporous network structure, demonstrating abundant water existed in the crosslinked structure of BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx. The presence of abundant hydroxyl groups in BC and GG led to the formation of strong intermolecular and intramolecular hydrogen bonding between the backbones of the two composites. When adding \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\) in the hydrogel solution, it quickly decomposed to \(\mathrm{B(OH_{4})^{-}}\) (Chen, Jiang, & Sun, 2014). These tetrahydroxy borate ions could form intermolecular hydrogen bonding with cis-diol hydroxyl groups in GG molecules and possibly form complex with hydroxyl groups in the two composites (Deka, Sarma, Patar, Gogoi, &
Sarmah, 2020), which facilitated the crosslinking of the hydrogel. Moreover, the prepared hydrogel exhibited remarkable self-healing ability as shown in Fig. 2b, the two pieces of hydrogel could be brought into one integral hydrogel without any external stimulus after 1 h. Such autonomous self-recovery behavior of hydrogels could be attributed to the reformation of hydrogen bonding across the interface and a reversible borax-diol recombination (Lu et al., 2017), which renders the hydrogel good flexibility.
## 3.3. Degradation behaviors and swelling analysis of BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/ GOx hydrogel
Borate ions crosslinked hydrogels were reported to perform pH-responsive (Tahdouhaisong, Boonha, Thammadwitian, Pattrumman, & Kulchat, 2020). As shown in Fig. 2c, GO hydrogel tended to decompose at acidic pH, which is due to the crosslinking between borate ions and hydroxyl groups is reversible. When at low pH, \(\mathrm{B(OH_{4})^{-}}\) converts to B \(\left(\mathrm{OH}\right)_{3}\) which leads to the breaking down of the borate crosslinking of the hydrogel, and this transition is reversible with pH increase (Deka et al., 2020). It is clearly that BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx hydrogel is stable even at pH 3, indicating the existence of BC provides a strong bond effect to improve the stability of the hydrogel against acid decomposition. Considering acidic pH is preferred for the POD-mimicking activity, the stability BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx in various pH condition was investigated in detail by measuring the weight percentage of the hydrogel. As shown in Fig. 2d, BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx hydrogel exhibited good stability at neutral pH value. With low BC content in the hydrogel, the weight
percentage gradually reduced with the decrease of pH value. With the increase of mass ratio of BC to GG, slight weight loss was observed even at pH 3, which further confirmed that the existence of BC could enhance the stability against acidic decomposition. Furthermore, the stability against enzymatic degradation of BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx hydrogel was evaluated by testing the weight loss in lysozyme solution since lysozyme is common enzyme existing in human body which may facilitate the degradation of hydrogels (Kim et al., 2018). BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx hydrogel showed slight weight loss after incubating in lysozyme solution for 10 days (Fig. 2e), indicating the good stability against enzymatic degradation. Therefore, the hydrogel is safe without additional contaminants while using in enzymatic condition.
Swelling ratio was tested to evaluate the water retention capacity. Fig. 2f showed that higher BC content in BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx hydrogel resulted in higher swelling ratio, and it reached highest swelling ratio of \(\sim\!3710\%\) in a mass ratio of 2:1 of BC:GG, demonstrating the excellent water retention capacity. These results could be ascribed to the strong bonding ability of BC with water. The freeze-dried hydrogel displayed plenty of micropore (Fig. 2a), which implies the abundant bound water in the prepared hydrogel. Interestingly, the dried BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx obtained after drying at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) could be recovered to hydrogel state after reswelling in water (Fig. 2g). The rearrangement of the crosslinked network after reswelling indicates the excellent crosslinking effect as well. The prominent water retention capacity of BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx hydrogel endows its potential biomedical applications to keep a moisture environment and absorb the exudates in applied sites.
## 3.4. Rheological analysis
The mechanical property of the hydrogels was evaluated by dynamic frequency sweep rheological test. As shown in Fig. 3a, for both BC/GG and BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx hydrogels, log \(\mathbf{G}^{\prime}\) was higher than log \(\mathbf{G}^{\prime\prime}\), demonstrating that the elastic behavior of the hydrogels is stronger than their viscous behavior, which performed as elastic solids. Furthermore, log \(\mathbf{G}^{\prime}\) value of BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx was a little higher than that of BC/ GG in the higher frequency region since Cu@ZIF/GOx nanoparticles improved the elastic behaviors of BC/GG hydrogel. Moreover, log \(\mathbf{G}^{\prime}\) of BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx hydrogel increased significantly with the increase of BC content in the hydrogel (Fig. 3b). The existence of BC enhanced the hydrogen bonding effect in the crosslinked networks, as well as improved the mechanical property by serving as reinforced scaffold in the hydrogel. These results indicated that the mechanical property of BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx hydrogel could be modulated by changing the content of BC and GG.
## 3.5. Catalytic performance analysis
MOF-based nanomaterials could be highly effective enzyme mimics due to their large surface area and regular distribution of active sites. Following a high-temperature pyrolysis procedure, Cu ions were encapsulated in the carbonizing MOF structure to construct a metal anchored carbon nanocatalyst, and these Cu ions serve as active sites which are expected to mimic enzyme catalytic activity. For Cu@ZIF/ GOx induced cascaded catalytic activity, we propose that the assembled GOx on the surface of Cu@ZIF could catalyze the abundant glucose to produce \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and gluconic acid, and the reduced pH value by gluconic acid could trigger the Cu-mediated POD-mimicking activity to produce •OH for inactivating bacteria. The POD-mimicking activity of Cu@ZIF was evaluated using typical peroxidase substrate TMB as the chromogenic substrate since it could be oxidized by the generated •OH to give a blue color in aqueous solution with maximum absorbance at \(652\,\mathrm{nm}\). As expected, the group of Cu@ZIF/GOx + glucose could effectively catalyze the oxidization of TMB after reacting for \(^{5\,\mathrm{h}}\) (Fig. 4a), indicating the generation of •OH, as well as proving the cascaded catalytic activity of Cu@ZIF/GOx. Particularly, distinct oxidized TMB peak was observed for BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx + glucose, suggesting the designed nanozyme encapsulated hydrogel could trigger the cascaded catalytic activity for further antibacterial application. For comparison, no absorbance peak at \(652\ \mathrm{nm}\) was observed in other control groups. Furthermore, the POD-mimicking activity of Cu@ZIF showed substrate dose-, pH- and temperature-dependent behaviors (Fig. S4). To further confirm the POD-mimicking ability of Cu@ZIF, we tested the fluorescence spectra using TA as fluorescence probe for •OH. Obviously, significant oxidized TA fluorescence at \(435\;\mathrm{nm}\) was detected for the groups of Cu@ZIF/GOx + glucose and BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx + glucose (Fig. 4b), while GOx + glucose showed no fluorescence at \(435\,\mathrm{nm}\), implying that Cu@ZIF performed as POD mimics to catalyze the generation of •OH. In addition, by using DMPO as the trapping agent of •OH, ESR spectra in Fig. 4c exhibited typical DMPO/•OH signal for the solution of \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF+H_{2}O_{2}}\) which was much stronger than the background signals, demonstrating that •OH was the main product from Cu@ZIF + \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) catalytic reaction, and further identified the POD-mimicking activity of Cu@ZIF.
To further verify the cascaded reaction catalyzed by Cu@ZIF/GOx, \(\mathrm{Ti}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})_{2}\) was used as the indicator to monitor the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) by testing the absorbance at \(405\;\mathrm{nm}\). Obviously, GOx could continuously catalyze the generation of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and reached an equilibrium within \(^{3\,\mathrm{h}}\), whereas the generated \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) was gradually decreased after \(^{3\,\mathrm{h}}\) catalyzed by Cu@ZIF/ GOx (Fig. 4d), indicating that the generated \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) was decomposed catalyzed by Cu@ZIF, as well as demonstrating that the cascaded reaction system provided appropriate pH for triggering the subsequent POD-mimicking activity. Furthermore, the pH value of the designed Cu@ZIF/
\(\mathrm{GOx+}\) glucose cascade reaction system was tested by a pH meter and using methyl red as pH indicator (yellow over pH of 6.2 and red under pH of 4.4) (Liu et al., 2019). As shown in Fig. 4e, the pH value of glucose solution was significantly reduced to final \(\mathsf{p H}\,3{-}4\) catalyzed by GOx, \(\mathsf{C u@Z I F/G O x}\) and BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx, which provides the essential pH value for activating the subsequent POD-mimicking activity. Besides, the pH kept decreasing within \(^{3\,\mathrm{h}}\) catalyzed by \(\mathsf{C u@Z I F/G O x}\) (Fig. 4f), indicating the continuous generation of glucose acid for reducing the pH.
GSH as the essential antioxidant extensively exists in bacteria to protect them from oxidative damage was reported to perform ROS scavenge ability (Fu, Wan, Qi, He, & Huang, 2021; Yan et al., 2013). Herein, the GSH consumption activity of \(\mathsf{C u@Z I F}\) was evaluated using DTNB as the tap agent of –SH in GSH. Characteristic absorbance at 412 nm with yellow color was observed for GSH (Fig. 4g). After incubating with \(\mathsf{C u@Z I F}\) , the absorbance peak disappeared, indicating the GSH depletion ability of \(\mathsf{C u@Z I F}\) . Meanwhile, the GSH depletion activity was related to the concentration of \(\mathsf{C u@Z I F}\) and reaction time (Fig. S5). In addition, such GSH depletion ability was highly oxygen-dependent (Fig. S6). To identify the ROS scavenge effect of GSH on \(\mathsf{C u@Z I F/}\)
\(\mathrm{GOx+}\) glucose catalytic system, GSH was added in the reaction solution. It was observed in Fig. 4h that the oxidation of TMB was strongly inhibited by GSH, and the POD-mimicking activity of \(\mathsf{C u@Z I F}\) was gradually weakened by the increase of GSH concentration (Fig. 4i), indicating that GSH could weaken the POD-mimicking activity for the generation of \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) . Thus, the GSH depletion ability of \(\mathsf{C u@Z I F}\) could enhance the cascaded reaction for the generation of \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) .
## 3.6. Antibacterial efficacy evaluation
The above revealed that both \(\mathsf{C u@Z I F/G O x}\) and BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/ GOx could catalyze the decomposition of glucose to in situ generate •OH via a self-activated cascaded catalytic activity. •OH as highly oxidative ROS could inactivate bacteria by oxidizing bacteria membrane and intracellular biomolecules such as proteins and lipids (Applerot et al., 2012; Li, Zhang, Niu, & Chen, 2012). We further evaluate the antibacterial efficacy of the prepared materials using a plate counting method. When treated with \(\mathsf{C u@Z I F/G O x}\) and BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx hydrogel without glucose, no antibacterial effect was observed compared with control group (Fig. 5a, b). With the presence of glucose,
\(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) and \(\mathrm{BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) could inactivate \(6.33\,\mathrm{log}\) (total) and \(4.13\,\mathrm{log}\) of S. aureus, and \(6.44\,\mathrm{log}\) (total) and \(4.94\,\mathrm{log}\) of E. coli, respectively. As comparison, GOx exhibited much lower antibacterial efficacy with \(1.73\,\mathrm{log}\) reduction of S. aureus and \(2\,\mathrm{log}\) reduction of E. coli, respectively. The generated \(\bullet\mathrm{OH}\) was responsible for such remarkable antibacterial efficacy. The corresponding bacterial colonies in Fig. 5c were in accordance with the above results. Due to the hydrophilic surface of \(\mathrm{BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx}\), the surrounding bacteria were easily adhered and proliferated on the hydrogel in the culture medium, and the captured bacteria could be effectively killed and removed from the hydrogel with the addition of glucose (Fig. S7). It is noting \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) performed better antibacterial efficacy than \(\mathrm{BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) hydrogel since \(\mathrm{BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) exhibited slower catalytic efficiency due to the encapsulation of \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) inside the hydrogel. However, the water-soluble GOx could continuously leach out to trigger the subsequent POD-mimicking activity. Time-dependent antibacterial efficacy of \(\mathrm{BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) was evaluated as shown in Fig. S8. Antibacterial efficacies with \(0.6\,\mathrm{log}\) reduction of S. aureus and \(1.1\,\mathrm{log}\) reduction of E. coli were observed in \(2\,\mathrm{h}\). With the extension of time, it exhibited significant improvement of antibacterial efficacies with \(3.1\,\mathrm{log}\) reduction of S. aureus and \(4.7\,\mathrm{log}\) reduction of E. coli in \(6\,\mathrm{h}\). The pH value was gradually decreased by the leached GOx for catalyzing the
performed better antibacterial efficacy than BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx hydrogel since BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx exhibited slower catalytic effi­ ciency due to the encapsulation of \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) inside the hydrogel. However, the water-soluble GOx could continuously leach out to trigger the subsequent POD-mimicking activity. Time-dependent antibacterial efficacy of \(\mathrm{BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) was evaluated as shown in Fig. S8. Antibacterial efficacies with \(0.6\,\mathrm{log}\) reduction of S. aureus and \(1.1\,\mathrm{log}\) reduction of E. coli were observed in \(2\,\mathrm{h}\) . With the extension of time, it exhibited significant improvement of antibacterial efficacies with 3.1 log reduction of S. aureus and 4.7 log reduction of E. coli in \(6\,\mathrm{h}\) . The pH value was gradually decreased by the leached GOx for catalyzing the
