decomposition of glucose, and then triggered the POD-mimicking activity.Glucose is the essential nutrition extensively exists in vivo, which was found in wound exudate as well (Trengove et al., 1996). Glucose concentration-dependent antibacterial efficacy of the prepared nanozyme and hydrogel was evaluated. When the concentration is below 3 mM, both \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) and \(\mathrm{BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) exhibited slight antibacterial effect (Fig. 5d–f), which was mainly due to the generated \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\). Clearly, they could effectively inactivate bacteria in the presence of physiological level of glucose in normal human blood \((3.9{-}7.8\ \mathrm{mM})\) and performed superior antibacterial efficiency with \(10\ \mathrm{mM}\) glucose (hyperglycemia level), indicating their potential application for bacteria-infectious diabetic wounds. With the increase of glucose concentration, pH value was gradually decreased owning to the generated glucose acid catalyzed by GOx, which activated the POD-mimicking activity of \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF}\). As a consequence, antibacterial efficacy was significantly improved by the generation of increasing amount of oxidative \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\). Glucose was demonstrated to accelerate the biofilm formation by constructing polysaccharide and massive bacteria complex (Croes et al., 2009; Trulear & Characklis, 1982), which is also responsible for nonhealing diabetic wounds. The stubborn biofilm could protect bacteria from killing by bactericides, results in the persistence of bacterial infections (Stewart, 2002). Based on the bacteria killing ability of \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) in glucose-containing environment, we further evaluated the biofilm eradication ability of the prepared nanozyme and hydrogels using a crystal violet staining method. As observed in Figs. 6a and b, the established biofilm treated with \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx+}\) glucose and \(\mathrm{BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx+}\) glucose was destroyed dramatically, and the corresponding relative absorbance at \(585\ \mathrm{nm}\) was obviously lower than other groups (PBS, \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx+PBS}\) and \(\mathrm{BC/GG+}\) glucose, \(\mathrm{GOx+}\) glucose). Due to the continuous glucose consumption catalyzed by
\(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) and strong oxidative damage of the generated \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) towards bacteria and polysaccharide in biofilm, the established biofilm was effectively destroyed. The results indicate that \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) and \(\mathrm{BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) are promising therapeutic materials for combating stubborn biofilm.
## 3.7. Biocompatibility evaluation in vitro
3.7. Biocompatibility evaluation in vitroThe results above demonstrated the impressive antibacterial property of the prepared nanozyme and hydrogel. To further explore their clinical application, cytocompatibility and hemocompatibility were evaluated in vitro. Cell viability of NIH-3T3 cells was evaluated using MTT assay to assess the cytocompatibility. As shown in Fig. 6c, cells after incubating with \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) in a glucose-free and \(\mathrm{BC/GOx}\) in glucose-containing media exhibited high cell viability. Correspondingly, the cells observed from light micrographs in Fig. 6d showed regular morphology with obvious proliferation amounts of cells, indicating their prominent cytocompatibility. With the addition of glucose in the culture medium, both \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) and \(\mathrm{BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) remained \(\sim80\%\) of cell viability, which is lower than other groups, indicating that the generated \(\cdot\mathrm{OH}\) had weak toxicity to normal cells. Furthermore, the hemolysis ratio was calculated to analyze the hemocompatibility. Obviously, both \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) and \(\mathrm{BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) displayed low relative hemolysis ratios under \(0.4\%\) (Fig. 7a), which is greatly lower than the critical blood-compatible range for biomaterials (lower than \(5\%\) ) according to ISO/TR 7406 (Wang, Wu, et al., 2021), demonstrating their excellent hemocompatibility.
## 3.8. In vitro and in vivo hemostatic assay
The whole blood clotting performance demonstrates the
thrombogenicity for biological application. The undesired bleeding in bacteria-infectious sites brings great difficulties for effective bacterial inactivation. Inspired by the noticeable antibacterial efficacy of the designed hydrogel with the assistance of physiological level of glucose in blood, the hemostasis behaviors were investigated. In vitro blood clotting potential of the developed nanozyme and hydrogels was evaluated by testing the BCI. As shown in Fig. 7b, \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\), BC/GG and BC/ \(\mathrm{GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) hydrogel exhibited significant blood clotting capacity with relative BCI below \(50\%\) , indicating their excellent hemostatic ability. The hemostatic mechanism was further investigated by observing the morphology of erythrocytes and platelets adhered on the surface of hydrogels (Fig. 7c). Surprisingly, plenty of erythrocytes aggregated on the surface for both BC/GG and BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx hydrogel. This adhesive property could be attributed to the superior water absorption capacity and hydrophilic property of the hydrogels. The accessible whole blood was likely to be absorbed by interconnected porous structure of hydrogels which led to the aggregation of erythrocytes on their surfaces, as well as accelerated the aggregation of platelets. As observed in Fig. 7c, numerous platelets with fibrin network were formed on the surface of the hydrogels, demonstrating the activation of platelets for promoting the formation of blood clot (Periayah, Halim, & Mat Saad, 2017), and evidently proved the precoagulant property of the prepared hydrogels. Besides, it formed macroscopic blood flocculates after mixing whole blood with BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx hydrogel (Fig. 7d), whereas whole blood exhibited a smooth and homogeneous fluxion without any blood clot, which further confirmed the hemostatic property of BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx hydrogel.
To explore the hemostatic capability of the nanozyme-based hydrogel in vivo, mouse liver hemorrhage model and mouse-tail amputation model were established. When applied BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx hydrogel on the bleeding sites of liver, it quickly stopped the bleeding with small amount of blood loss on the filter paper, while control rats without
hydrogel experienced more amount of blood loss with continuous bleeding in \(7~\mathrm{min}\) (Fig. 8a–c). In addition, the results from mouse-tail amputation model confirmed the rapid hemostatic capacity of BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx as well (Fig. 8d–f). When applied the hydrogel on bleeding sites, water from serum was rapidly absorbed by the hydrogel to concentrate clotting factors and accelerate fibrin formation and platelet activation (Fathi, Sikorski, Christodoulides, Langan, & Kofinas, 2018), resulted in the above hemostatic performance in vivo.
## 4. Conclusions
A nanozyme-based hydrogel with self-healing, ROS regulation and superior water retention capacity was constructed for synergetic antibacterial and hemostatic application. The designed Cu doped MOF nanocatalyst assembling with GOx could effectively catalyze the decomposition of glucose and exhibited remarkable POD-mimicking and GSH consumption ability to mediate ROS level for antibacterial application. Based on the multienzyme mimetic activity of \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\), the prepared BC/GG-Cu@ZIF/GOx hydrogel exhibited desired catalytic activity to convert physiological level of glucose to high oxidative \(\mathrm{OH}\) via a self-activated cascaded reaction, and displayed noticeable antibacterial efficacy in the presence of normal level and hyperglycemia level of glucose in human blood \((4{-}10\,\mathrm{mM})\), which could eliminate the stubborn biofilm and inhibit the formation of biofilm as well by consuming the glucose in biofilm. Additionally, the prepared hydrogel exhibited high swelling ratio for absorbing water, excellent biocompatibility and rapid hemostatic property both in vitro and in vivo. In terms of the absorption behavior of the hydrogel resulted in the rapid hemostasis, \(\mathrm{Cu@ZIF/GOx}\) encapsulated in the hydrogel could convert the glucose in blood to \(\mathrm{OH}\) for bacterial killing simultaneously. This work provides a novel strategy to achieve synergetic hemostasis and bacterial killing via a glucose-responsive catalytic cascade reaction, is of great potential in
biomedical application.
